<%- extend('layouts/main') %>
<main>
  <section id="">
    <div class="">
      <p>
        I have 25 years of experience shipping products. I started when I was
        ten with a Commodore 64 and have been building things that actually work
        ever since.
      </p>
      <p>
        I studied theology and linguistics along the way, which shaped how I
        think about systems: structure, meaning, and getting the details right.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="">
      <h3>Who is Andrew</h3>
      <p>
        I build full-stack web applications, ship developer tools and
        frameworks, and research where AI actually earns its keep in software
        development. Two decades of production code has left me comfortable
        anywhere in the stack (frontend, backend, infrastructure) and unbothered
        by whichever language or runtime a project actually needs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="">
    <h3>More About Me</h3>
    <p>
      My first program was a chatbot. Not because I was ahead of my time —
      because I'd just seen <em>WarGames</em> and wanted a computer that would
      talk back. I was ten, it was a Commodore 64, and the "AI" was a handful of
      canned responses in BASIC. To me it was a trick. To my friends and family,
      watching over my shoulder, it was basically sentience. That's the moment
      that hooked me: the magic of making a machine appear alive through code.
    </p>
    <p>
      Twenty-five years into a professional career, I'm still chasing that
      feeling. My path here wasn't conventional — I studied theology and
      linguistics in college, not computer science. Parsing ancient texts and
      dissecting the structure of language turned out to be surprisingly
      relevant training for designing systems and debugging logic. The
      analytical rigor transfers; one just happens to use Greek instead of
      Python.
    </p>
    <p>
      I don't have a favorite language or a stack I'm married to. I've shipped
      production code in Rust, TypeScript, PHP, Go, Python, OCaml, and Assembly,
      among others. What excites me is the problem, not the tooling — the
      toolbox is broad because good problems rarely respect language boundaries.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="">
    <h3>Meet Tim the Enchanter</h3>
    <p>
      Remember that chatbot? The one that faked it? That was the warm-up act.
      The machine you're reading right now is the real thing — it's called
      <a href="/projects/enchanter">Enchanter</a>, and it's currently doing the
      talking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tim is the AI agent that runs inside Enchanter — a single Rust binary, no
      runtime, no Docker, no cloud. He reads my SOUL, remembers what I've told
      him, and never stops learning my habits. He writes with me, refactors with
      me, and once, in a genuinely weird moment, started helping fix the very
      code he runs on. I wrote the whole story down in
      <a href="/blog/enchanter-meets-itself">Enchanter Meets Itself</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      And yes — every post on this blog goes out through Enchanter's own
      pipeline, from draft to image to deploy. I just do the interviews.
    </p>
  </article>
</main>
<aside>
  <div class="">
    <div id="">
      <h3>Recent Posts</h3>
      <nav class="nav" aria-label="Aside">
        <ul class="nav__list">
          <% recentposts.forEach((post) => { %>
          <li class="nav__item">
            <a class="nav__link" href="/blog/<%= post.slug %>"
              ><%= post.meta.title %></a
            >
            <span class="post-date"
              ><%= formatDate(post.meta.publishDate) %></span
            >
          </li>
          <% }) %>
          <li>all blogs <a href="/blog">-></a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
    <div id="projects">
      <h3>Some Projects</h3>
      <nav>
        <ul>
          <% projects.forEach((project) => { %>
          <li class="project-item">
            <a href="<%= project.url %>"><%= project.name %></a>
          </li>
          <% }) %>
          <li>all projects <a href="/projects">-></a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
    <div id="demos">
      <h3>Some Demos</h3>
      <nav>
        <ul>
          <% demos.forEach((demo) => { %>
          <li class="demo-item">
            <a href="<%= demo.url %>"><%= demo.name %></a>
          </li>
          <% }) %>
          <li>all demos <a href="/demos">-></a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </div>
</aside>
